<div id="alg2.6.19" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="alg2.6.19.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">  </span><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span><span id="alg2.6.19.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">  append </span><math id="alg2.m24" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathrm{reshape}\left(\cdot\rightarrow shape_{normalized}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">reshape</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="0.800em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="0.800em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">s</mi><mi mathsize="0.800em">h</mi><mi mathsize="0.800em">a</mi><mi mathsize="0.800em">p</mi><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">e</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">z</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">d</mi></mrow></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{reshape}\left(\cdot\rightarrow shape_{normalized}\right)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg2.6.19.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;"> to </span><math id="alg2.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{Ops}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.800em">𝖮𝗉𝗌</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{Ops}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg2.6.19.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg2.6.20" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="alg2.6.20.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">append </span><math id="alg2.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{transpose}\left(p\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">transpose</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">p</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{transpose}\left(p\right)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg2.6.20.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;"> to </span><math id="alg2.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{Ops}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.800em">𝖮𝗉𝗌</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{Ops}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg2.6.20.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;"> </span>
</div>
<div id="alg2.6.21" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="alg2.6.21.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;">if</span><span id="alg2.6.21.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;"> </span><em id="alg2.6.21.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic" style="font-size:80%;"><math id="alg2.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{E}_{\text{d}}.shape\neq{{E}_{\text{b}}}.shape" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>E</mi><mo mathsize="1.250em">^</mo></mover><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">d</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo>≠</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">b</mtext></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{E}_{\text{d}}.shape\neq{{E}_{\text{b}}}.shape</annotation></semantics></math></em><span id="alg2.6.21.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;"> </span><span id="alg2.6.21.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;">then</span>
</div>
<div id="alg2.6.22" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="alg2.6.22.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">  </span><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span><span id="alg2.6.22.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">  append </span><math id="alg2.m29" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathrm{reshape}\left(\cdot\rightarrow{E}_{\text{b}}.shape\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">reshape</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="0.800em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="0.800em" stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">E</mi><mtext mathsize="0.800em">b</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="0.800em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">s</mi><mi mathsize="0.800em">h</mi><mi mathsize="0.800em">a</mi><mi mathsize="0.800em">p</mi><mi mathsize="0.800em">e</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{reshape}\left(\cdot\rightarrow{E}_{\text{b}}.shape\right)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg2.6.22.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;"> to </span><math id="alg2.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{Ops}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.800em">𝖮𝗉𝗌</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{Ops}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg2.6.22.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg2.6.23" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="alg2.6.23.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;">return</span><span id="alg2.6.23.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;"> </span><math id="alg2.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{Ops}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.800em">𝖮𝗉𝗌</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{Ops}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg2.6.23.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">
</span>
</div>
</div>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS2.SSS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.SSS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Scope assumptions. </span>
The numerous equivalent and different sequence layout transformations make it computationally challenging to infer a bijection mapping that converts one sequence into another.
<span id="S5.SS2.SSS3.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Scalify</span> limits the scope of the algorithm to reshape operations that merges or split dimensions within a tensor. This is because we find that many production-grade ML frameworks reshape the tensors on a grouping mechanism. Figure <a href="#S5.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ 5.2. Processing: Incremental E-Graph Construction ‣ 5. System Design ‣ Verifying Computational Graphs in Production-Grade Distributed Machine Learning Frameworks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> has several examples where the reshape operation is either a splitting or merging of dimensions (<em id="S5.SS2.SSS3.p4.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g.,</em> reshape from (256, 4096) to (64, 4, 4096)).</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.3. </span>Postprocessing: Localizing Code Bugs based on E-Graph Discrepancies</h3>

<div id="S5.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our tool
should not only give the binary result (verified/unverified) but also aid developers
in locating the bugs.
To facilitate this, <span id="S5.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Scalify</span> instruments the ML compiler using the tool logging API
to capture source-level
debugging information during graph generation.
<span id="S5.SS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Scalify</span> stores metadata
and associates source AST nodes with IR graph nodes when constructing the IR graphs from the source code.
Each IR graph node is extended with a unique identifier,
which points to the source file, function, and line number,
<em id="S5.SS3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g.,</em> <span id="S5.SS3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">{source line: ‘‘flash_decoding.py:42’’, expr: "hlo.exp(...)"}</span>,
which is preserved at nodes when converting IR graphs to e-graphs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">During the rewrite process, <span id="S5.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Scalify</span> checks whether the node <span id="S5.SS3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Scalify</span> rewrites belongs to a relation or not, and stores each node into two categories: verified and unverified.
Generating a list of unverified nodes is not helpful since in non-equivalent graphs, the list of unverified nodes can be numerous, and the source of the issue is not the unverified node.
When <span id="S5.SS3.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Scalify</span> detects non-equivalence,
<span id="S5.SS3.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Scalify</span> goes through every unverified node and checks whether the inputs of that node is verified or not.
<span id="S5.SS3.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Scalify</span> generates unverified nodes where the inputs are verified, also with the corresponding source line and file number.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F10" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.10694v1/localization_v2.svg" id="S5.F10.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:229/144;" width="229" height="144"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F10.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 10</span>. </span><span id="S5.F10.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Upper graph is the oracle and bottom graph is the buggy graph. The layout relation does not propagate because the bijections of the inputs of add are not the same. </span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example.</span>
As shown in Figure <a href="#S5.F10" title="Figure 10 ‣ 5.3. Postprocessing: Localizing Code Bugs based on E-Graph Discrepancies ‣ 5. System Design ‣ Verifying Computational Graphs in Production-Grade Distributed Machine Learning Frameworks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>, the buggy <span id="S5.SS3.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">add</span> operators are unverified because their input layout relations mismatch: the <span id="S5.SS3.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">reshape</span> and <span id="S5.SS3.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">allreduce add</span> align via <span id="S5.SS3.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">[transpose(2, 0, 1), reshape(4096, 64, 4)]</span>, while tensors <math id="S5.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> align via a different transpose <span id="S5.SS3.p3.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">(2, 1, 0)</span>. This mismatch prevents <span id="S5.SS3.p3.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Scalify</span> from reasoning about the <span id="S5.SS3.p3.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">add</span> nodes. <span id="S5.SS3.p3.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Scalify</span> then reports these unverified <span id="S5.SS3.p3.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">add</span> nodes and their consumers, highlighting verified inputs and revealing that the true issue originates from the <span id="S5.SS3.p3.1.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">mlp_hidden</span> variable after its <span id="S5.SS3.p3.1.12" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">reshape</span>. Thus, the <span id="S5.SS3.p3.1.13" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">add</span> node is pinpointed as the root of the discrepancy: its inputs are verified, but the node itself fails to align.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6. </span>Implementation</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We implement <span id="S6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Scalify</span> primarily in Python with approximately 9k SLOC,
of which about 6.5K LOC are devoted to manually encoding 25 meta rules across different forms of parallelism (<em id="S6.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g.</em>, tensor, expert) and supporting the corresponding IR graph operators. We found the defined rules highly reusable across different semantic types.
Once the framework and the basic rule set are in place, adding rule support for new parallelism techniques requires only modest effort. For example, we added 2 rules to enable sequence parallelism, which are implemented in 30 LOC.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The core layout-equivalence reasoning is powered by egglog, which we integrate as the e-graph engine for tensor relation resolution.
<span id="S6.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Scalify</span> is built on top of PyTorch XLA to operate directly on the intermediate representation of ML models. Our prototype targets AWS Transformers NeuronX <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">tra, 2025</a>)</cite> and NeuronX Distributed <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">nxd, 2025</a>)</cite> from the AWS Neuron SDK, though the core algorithms are framework-agnostic and could be ported to other IR-based systems (<em id="S6.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g.,</em> TensorFlow XLA, Megatron-LM) with moderate effort.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">7. </span>Evaluation</h2>

<div id="S7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We answer these questions in our evaluation:
(1) Does <span id="S7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Scalify</span> support real-world large ML models (§<a href="#S7.SS1" title="7.1. Verifying Large Real-world Models ‣ 7. Evaluation ‣ Verifying Computational Graphs in Production-Grade Distributed Machine Learning Frameworks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.1</span></a>)?
(2) Does <span id="S7.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Scalify</span> scale well as the size of models increases (§<a href="#S7.SS2" title="7.2. Scalability ‣ 7. Evaluation ‣ Verifying Computational Graphs in Production-Grade Distributed Machine Learning Frameworks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.2</span></a>)?
(3) Does <span id="S7.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Scalify</span> detect any bugs in the real-world systems and what categories of bugs does <span id="S7.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Scalify</span> cover (§<a href="#S7.SS3" title="7.3. Bugs Detection ‣ 7. Evaluation ‣ Verifying Computational Graphs in Production-Grade Distributed Machine Learning Frameworks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.3</span></a>)?</p>
</div>
<section id="S7.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">7.1. </span>Verifying Large Real-world Models</h3>

<div id="S7.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Setup.</span>
The graphs are generated using the XLA compiler on AWS Trainium machines with 32 Neuron cores (Transformers NeuronX, version 0.12.313). Verification is performed on a 6-core AMD Ryzen 5 5600U CPU with 16 GB RAM.
Note that our evaluation is conducted on inference graphs generated by NeuronX. This choice is due to current framework support, not a limitation of <span id="S7.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Scalify</span> itself.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate our tool on two popular LLMs: Llama-3, representing dense models, and Mixtral, representing Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) architectures. We run the models with the parallelization pipelines commonly used by developers, including tensor parallelism. For Llama-3, we additionally verify equivalence under flash decoding and sequence parallelism, while for Mixtral we check expert parallelism implemented with recursive loops. In total, our evaluation covers four parallelism techniques. All generated graphs are executed on 32 Neuron cores, the largest configuration supported by NeuronX.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S7.T2" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S7.T2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 2</span>. </span><span id="S7.T2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Evaluated real-world large models.</span></figcaption>
<table id="S7.T2.5" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S7.T2.5.1" class="ltx_tr">
