<td id="S7.T2.5.6.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="S7.T2.5.6.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">Mixtral-8x22B</span></td>
<td id="S7.T2.5.6.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="S7.T2.5.6.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">56</span></td>
<td id="S7.T2.5.6.4" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="S7.T2.5.6.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">3m 1s</span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
<div id="S7.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Results.</span>
We show the results in Table <a href="#S7.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 7.1. Verifying Large Real-world Models ‣ 7. Evaluation ‣ Verifying Computational Graphs in Production-Grade Distributed Machine Learning Frameworks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. All verification completes within a few minutes on a commodity machine, showing the practicality of our tool.
<span id="S7.SS1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Scalify</span> takes longer time for Mixtral compared to Llama-3.1 since Mixtral models have more nodes and more fine-grained analysis (unroll loop of recursive add operations for expert parallelism).
Unlike TrainVerify <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lu
et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, which reasons at the per-element level across devices and takes days even for Llama-3.1-405B, <span id="S7.SS1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Scalify</span> reasons over distributed tensors as sharded entities, yielding orders-of-magnitude faster runtimes.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">7.2. </span>Scalability</h3>

<div id="S7.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate the performance of <span id="S7.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Scalify</span> against 5 groups of parallelization and model configurations. The base model is the Llama-3.1-8B and the distributed graph is run across 32 neuron cores on AWS trainium machines. Table <a href="#S7.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 7.2. Scalability ‣ 7. Evaluation ‣ Verifying Computational Graphs in Production-Grade Distributed Machine Learning Frameworks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> represents the control variable and inference configurations for each group. The graphs are generated from Transformers NeuronX.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S7.T3" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S7.T3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 3</span>. </span><span id="S7.T3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Configurations for scalability experiments.</span></figcaption>
