</figure>
<figure id="S7.F11" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.10694v1/various_conf.svg" id="S7.F11.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/85;" width="476" height="85"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S7.F11.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 11</span>. </span><span id="S7.F11.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps" style="font-size:90%;">Scalify</span><span id="S7.F11.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">’s performance when checking the equivalence for different configurations. </span></figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S7.F12" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.10694v1/scale.svg" id="S7.F12.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:381/179;" width="381" height="179"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S7.F12.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 12</span>. </span><span id="S7.F12.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Verification time based on scaling techniques. </span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S7.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Constant relationship to tensor shapes</span>. Shown in Figure <a href="#S7.F11" title="Figure 11 ‣ 7.2. Scalability ‣ 7. Evaluation ‣ Verifying Computational Graphs in Production-Grade Distributed Machine Learning Frameworks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>a, <a href="#S7.F11" title="Figure 11 ‣ 7.2. Scalability ‣ 7. Evaluation ‣ Verifying Computational Graphs in Production-Grade Distributed Machine Learning Frameworks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>b and <a href="#S7.F11" title="Figure 11 ‣ 7.2. Scalability ‣ 7. Evaluation ‣ Verifying Computational Graphs in Production-Grade Distributed Machine Learning Frameworks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>e, the verification time is the same regardless of the size of the input tensor shapes. This is because <span id="S7.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Scalify</span> operates on the computation graph level, where the size of the expression stays the same regardless of the tensor shape.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Constant relationship to parallelization degree</span>. Shown in Figure <a href="#S7.F11" title="Figure 11 ‣ 7.2. Scalability ‣ 7. Evaluation ‣ Verifying Computational Graphs in Production-Grade Distributed Machine Learning Frameworks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>d, the verification time of <span id="S7.SS2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Scalify</span> is constant to the complexity of the parallelization since the size of the generated computation graph is the same regardless of the number of cores used to run the ML pipeline. The additional communication nodes introduced through these parallelization has a negligible effect to the total verification time.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Linear relationship to number of layers</span>. Shown in Figure <a href="#S7.F11" title="Figure 11 ‣ 7.2. Scalability ‣ 7. Evaluation ‣ Verifying Computational Graphs in Production-Grade Distributed Machine Learning Frameworks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>c, increasing model layers causes the operators to be added sequentially, which causes the verification time of <span id="S7.SS2.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Scalify</span> to increase. The additional time is introduced due to the partitioning of graph into multiple subgraphs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Graph partitioning and layer memoization verification</span>. We demonstrate the benefit of layer memoization by comparing it against sequential graph partitioning and parallel rewrite with graph partitioning as shown in Figure <a href="#S7.F12" title="Figure 12 ‣ 7.2. Scalability ‣ 7. Evaluation ‣ Verifying Computational Graphs in Production-Grade Distributed Machine Learning Frameworks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>. We run the experiments on Llama-3.1-8B model with TP degree of 32 and 32 layers.
We tried running the tool against the base Llama-3.1-8B model without graph partitioning and our tool fails to rewrite the entire model due to insufficient resources.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">7.3. </span>Bugs Detection</h3>

<div id="S7.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">First, we categorize classes of silent errors into the following categories that can be detected by <span id="S7.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Scalify</span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ol id="S7.I1" class="ltx_enumerate ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="S7.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S7.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Incorrect distributed operation</span>: Using the wrong communication primitive (<em id="S7.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g.,</em> an unnecessary all-reduce).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S7.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S7.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Incorrect distributed configuration</span>: Incorrect device assignment even when the operator itself is correct (<em id="S7.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g.,</em> reducing on only part of the cores).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S7.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S7.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Inconsistent tensor precision</span>: Single-device and distributed pipelines use different precisions.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S7.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(4)</span> 
<div id="S7.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Incorrect axis splitting</span>: Reshape operations split tensors incorrectly, breaking sharding relations.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S7.I1.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(5)</span> 
<div id="S7.I1.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.I1.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.I1.i5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Incorrect layout optimization</span>: An invalid sequence of layout transformations compared to the oracle.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Categories 1-4 are covered by <span id="S7.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Scalify</span>’s partition and layout analysis, which focuses on propagating relations through intermediate tensors whenever the tensors match the rules defined in Table <a href="#S5.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 5.2.2. Propagating Relations with Layout and Partition Analysis ‣ 5.2. Processing: Incremental E-Graph Construction ‣ 5. System Design ‣ Verifying Computational Graphs in Production-Grade Distributed Machine Learning Frameworks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.
Category 5 is eliminated through <span id="S7.SS3.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Scalify</span>’s bijection inference.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S7.T4" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S7.T4.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 4</span>. </span><span id="S7.T4.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Reproduced old bugs detected by <span id="S7.T4.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Scalify</span>. ➸: pinpoint the faulty instr. ✻: pinpoint the faulty function or data structure.
n/a: not applicable (undetected).
</span></figcaption>
