<td id="S7.T4.6.10.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="S7.T4.6.10.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">Bug#19</span></td>
<td id="S7.T4.6.10.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb"><span id="S7.T4.6.10.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">Incorrect logits layout</span></td>
<td id="S7.T4.6.10.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S7.T4.6.10.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">(</span><a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">tra, [n.d.]c</a><span id="S7.T4.6.10.3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">)</span></cite></td>
<td id="S7.T4.6.10.4" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb"><span id="S7.T4.6.10.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">n/a</span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
<div id="S7.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Setup.</span>
We evaluate <span id="S7.SS3.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Scalify</span> on incorrect distributed computation graphs by reproducing 19 real-world bug cases across diverse ML systems, re-implemented in Transformers NeuronX.
These bugs represent real-world silent errors that are frequently encountered.
We use the Llama-3.1-8B model as workloads with TP degree of 32.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Finding reproduced old bugs.</span>
The result is shown in Table <a href="#S7.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ 7.3. Bugs Detection ‣ 7. Evaluation ‣ Verifying Computational Graphs in Production-Grade Distributed Machine Learning Frameworks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>.
17 out of 19 reproduced bugs are detected by <span id="S7.SS3.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Scalify</span> under one minute. Besides detection, we also show how the output pinpoints the bug in the source code.
We already showed Bug#1 in Figure <a href="#S5.F10" title="Figure 10 ‣ 5.3. Postprocessing: Localizing Code Bugs based on E-Graph Discrepancies ‣ 5. System Design ‣ Verifying Computational Graphs in Production-Grade Distributed Machine Learning Frameworks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a> and explained in §<a href="#S5.SS3" title="5.3. Postprocessing: Localizing Code Bugs based on E-Graph Discrepancies ‣ 5. System Design ‣ Verifying Computational Graphs in Production-Grade Distributed Machine Learning Frameworks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.3</span></a>.
For Bug#3–8, <span id="S7.SS3.p5.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Scalify</span> pinpoints the faulty function but not the missing operation required. For instance, in missing all-reduce bugs,
<span id="S7.SS3.p5.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Scalify</span> flags add operators which consumes partial and sharded tensors (<span id="S7.SS3.p5.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">hlo.add(mlp_hidden, hidden)</span>) because
the input tensors have different relations.
Bug#18–19 cannot be detected by <span id="S7.SS3.p5.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Scalify</span> since the bug manifests outside of the graph compilation phase.</p>
</div>
